WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00.230 --> 00:10.050
In her TED Talk, the astronomer Merced uses a range of stylistic techniques to bring a very complex, very abstract subject vividly to life.

2
00:10.890 --> 00:14.690
What does she do? She starts out by telling a story.

3
00:15.170 --> 00:19.490
It’s almost like a children’s story. “Once there was a star. She was born…”

4
00:20.290 --> 00:26.400
On it goes, charting the life of this star, the death, and then the rebirth as a supernova.

5
00:27.720 --> 00:33.920
It’s a way of making something almost beyond our comprehension comprehensible. She makes it real. She makes it human.

6
00:34.730 --> 00:42.266
That kicks her off. Then she gets into the more technical part of the story, where she’s talking about the energy, the scale, the dimensions.

7
00:42.266 --> 00:44.030
She compares the amount of energy

8
00:44.450 --> 00:48.550
to the amount of energy that the sun burns in ten days.

9
00:49.350 --> 00:56.230
That helps us conceive of something that we could never understand if she told us how many trillion kilojoules it was.

10
00:57.290 --> 00:59.810
Similarly, she compares the magnetic field

11
01:00.450 --> 01:06.150
to that of the Earth. I think it was a thousand trillion times bigger than that of the Earth.

12
01:06.920 --> 01:10.400
And that helps us conceive of these huge numbers in a way

13
01:10.920 --> 01:14.634
the actual number itself would be meaningless for us.

14
01:14.634 --> 01:21.150
So she starts using the stylistic techniques of her physical presence, of her vocal emphasis,

15
01:21.700 --> 01:27.330
to bring this stuff to life, to underline where the important points in her message are,

16
01:28.040 --> 01:31.620
because it’s difficult stuff she’s talking about. We need her to help us in this way.

17
01:32.580 --> 01:39.460
Think about how she puts the vocal emphasis in her sentences on particular words. “A tremendous amount of energy.”

18
01:40.900 --> 01:44.190
“An itty-bitty, tiny portion of the electromagnetic spectrum,”

19
01:44.680 --> 01:52.000
and that was matched to a tiny little gesture. And then there are the big gestures. She talks about the spinning star, spinning

20
01:52.390 --> 01:57.380
faster and faster, and she uses, with her words,

21
01:57.730 --> 02:01.920
the analogy of the ice skater, and she illustrates that with her arms. We see it happening.

22
02:02.850 --> 02:06.710
And then she talks about the whirling matter being dragged around.

23
02:06.710 --> 02:14.430
So she’s bringing it to life using vocal emphasis, using physical gestures, and using vivid analogies and compelling simple stories.

